---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A lecture theatre reduced to flat gold and black shapes, rows of seats sweeping past the frame, in a two-ink risograph print"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    You will observe, accommodate, and negotiate with the arthropods that
    already share your dwelling, and you will do so with citations. Each
    week pairs a spatial or behavioural question — what a silverfish
    actually consumes when it eats a book's binding glue, why a corner
    web is placed where it is, what a crumb trail tells you about an ant
    scout's search algorithm — with the peer-reviewed literature that has
    already measured it. You will track a single identified household
    insect for five days without interfering, and map what its territory
    reveals about your home's ecosystem. You will design a non-disruptive
    architectural modification that measurably improves a resident
    species' quality of life. You will end the semester drafting a
    binding, reciprocal lease agreement between the human leaseholder and
    the arthropod populations sharing the dwelling. The artefact you leave
    with is not a body count; it is a tenancy you can defend.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    This course is for anyone who has found an insect indoors and reached
    for a shoe before asking what it was doing there. It assumes no
    entomology, no architecture, and no prior fieldwork — only a
    willingness to treat a corner web as a design decision rather than a
    mess. It is not for anyone hoping for irony: the premise is absurd,
    the method is not, and the two are never allowed to acknowledge each
    other.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The weekly schedule and what happens in each teaching session.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
